WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:09.350
Earlier in this lesson, 100 helpers counted a million peas into one pot, all at the same time.

2
00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:12.377
A plain variable lost about 17,000 of them.

3
00:00:12.377 --> 00:00:17.516
Three tools counted every pea: an atomic, a Mutex, and a one-thread view.

4
00:00:17.667 --> 00:00:20.108
That last one looks like the cheap fix.

5
00:00:20.108 --> 00:00:23.176
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

6
00:00:23.176 --> 00:00:28.247
If only one coroutine runs at a time, how can two of them still lose an addition?

7
00:00:31.667 --> 00:00:33.164
Let's go to the kitchen.

8
00:00:33.164 --> 00:00:39.154
Before a big dinner, a shopping list hangs on the fridge, and helpers write down what's missing.

9
00:00:39.154 --> 00:00:43.335
The house has one rule: only one person may hold the pen at a time.

10
00:00:43.533 --> 00:00:44.409
Let's map it.

11
00:00:44.409 --> 00:00:50.069
Each helper is a coroutine, a piece of work that can pause without holding a thread.

12
00:00:50.069 --> 00:00:52.023
The list is the shared state.

13
00:00:52.023 --> 00:01:01.523
And the house rule is a view like limitedParallelism(1), which runs one coroutine at a time: holding the pen means running on its one thread.

14
00:01:01.667 --> 00:01:02.847
Now slow it down.

15
00:01:02.847 --> 00:01:05.001
The list says flour, 200 grams.

16
00:01:05.001 --> 00:01:07.570
Helper A takes the pen and reads 200.

17
00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:09.862
How many grams is one more scoop?

18
00:01:09.862 --> 00:01:14.585
She asks the recipe app, and while she waits, she puts the pen down.

19
00:01:14.733 --> 00:01:18.557
Helper B picks it up, reads 200 too, and asks the app.

20
00:01:18.557 --> 00:01:23.159
A gets her answer, 100 grams, takes the pen back, and writes 300.

21
00:01:23.159 --> 00:01:25.141
Then B gets the same answer.

22
00:01:25.141 --> 00:01:26.557
What does she write?

23
00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:30.688
300, the same as A.

24
00:01:30.688 --> 00:01:33.062
Two scoops needed, one on the list.

25
00:01:33.062 --> 00:01:37.199
And nobody broke the rule: only one helper ever held the pen.

26
00:01:37.199 --> 00:01:42.013
The rule was followed perfectly, and the cake will still come out flat.

27
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:45.746
So the point is, the rule guards the pen, not the addition.

28
00:01:45.746 --> 00:01:47.909
And the pen went down in the middle.

29
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:52.437
Here's that addition, as the lab's ShoppingList starts out.

30
00:01:52.437 --> 00:01:54.040
toGrams is the recipe app.

31
00:01:54.040 --> 00:02:02.239
It's a suspend function, so the helper may pause there, at a suspension point, and while she's paused, the view runs the next helper.

32
00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:10.234
The library's own documentation has a heading for this: limitedParallelism(1) is not a mutex, with an exclamation mark.

33
00:02:10.234 --> 00:02:13.592
When the docs start shouting, it's worth listening.

34
00:02:14.333 --> 00:02:18.340
The lesson's NotAMutex runs three helpers on a one-thread view.

35
00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:22.028
Each prints read, waits 20 milliseconds, and prints write.

36
00:02:22.028 --> 00:02:24.572
In what order do the six lines come out?

37
00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.733
All three read before anyone wrote.

38
00:02:29.733 --> 00:02:32.000
Three additions, and two are lost.

39
00:02:33.533 --> 00:02:37.604
What we want is a helper who keeps the pen while she asks the app.

40
00:02:37.604 --> 00:02:39.762
That's a Mutex, the coroutine lock.

41
00:02:39.762 --> 00:02:44.635
withLock takes it, runs the block, and gives it back, even if the block throws.

42
00:02:44.635 --> 00:02:47.533
The holder still pauses, but she keeps the pen.

43
00:02:47.733 --> 00:02:55.051
The others wait without holding a thread, since waiting for a Mutex suspends, and they get the pen in the order they asked.

44
00:02:55.051 --> 00:02:59.096
Under withLock, each helper reads and writes before the next begins.

45
00:03:02.133 --> 00:03:04.368
The lab's solution goes one better.

46
00:03:04.368 --> 00:03:09.412
Ask the app before you take the pen, then read and write with no pause between.

47
00:03:09.412 --> 00:03:11.711
The lookups overlap, so it's faster.

48
00:03:12.467 --> 00:03:13.270
Now the name.

49
00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:19.635
Code that must run with nothing else touching the same state in the middle of it is a critical section.

50
00:03:19.635 --> 00:03:21.798
And here's the rule for coroutines.

51
00:03:21.798 --> 00:03:26.680
Every suspension point is a middle, even one hidden inside a function you call.

52
00:03:27.467 --> 00:03:28.370
Two warnings.

53
00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:35.664
A Mutex isn't reentrant: a helper who holds it and asks again waits forever, for the pen in her own hand.

54
00:03:35.664 --> 00:03:40.180
In NotAMutex, the nested withLock gave up after 500 milliseconds.

55
00:03:40.333 --> 00:03:42.217
And a lock has a price.

56
00:03:42.217 --> 00:03:47.949
A lock for every single pea took 891 milliseconds, the atomic took 43.

57
00:03:47.949 --> 00:03:49.750
One number: an atomic.

58
00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:52.371
Several steps together: a Mutex.

59
00:03:53.133 --> 00:03:54.547
The list is safe now.

60
00:03:54.547 --> 00:04:01.147
Proving it in a test is harder: every lookup takes time, and real waits make tests slow and shaky.

61
00:04:01.333 --> 00:04:06.431
A theatre solves this with a rehearsal: nobody waits three hours for the roast.

62
00:04:06.431 --> 00:04:08.109
runTest is that rehearsal.

63
00:04:08.109 --> 00:04:14.111
It skips every delay, and a virtual clock, a pretend one, jumps to the next delay that's due.

64
00:04:14.267 --> 00:04:18.044
The lesson's VirtualTime holds a rehearsal dinner.

65
00:04:18.044 --> 00:04:23.784
The soup simmers 1,000 milliseconds and seasons 200, then bread bakes 2,000.

66
00:04:23.784 --> 00:04:27.335
Meanwhile, the roast cooks 3,000 and rests 500.

67
00:04:27.335 --> 00:04:29.525
In what order do they finish?

68
00:04:32.667 --> 00:04:40.452
Soup at 1,000 and 1,200, roast at 3,000, bread at 3,200, and the rested roast at 3,500.

69
00:04:40.452 --> 00:04:48.595
Three and a half seconds of dinner, in about 20 real milliseconds after the library loaded.

70
00:04:48.733 --> 00:04:50.394
Here the picture breaks.

71
00:04:50.394 --> 00:04:53.646
The stage manager can only skip scenes he runs.

72
00:04:53.646 --> 00:05:00.497
A delay on Dispatchers.Default stays real: a real second passed, and the virtual clock stayed at 0.

73
00:05:00.497 --> 00:05:02.435
So pass your dispatchers in.

74
00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:07.816
Here's the fixed addition in Kotlin, with Java's ReentrantLock below it.

75
00:05:07.816 --> 00:05:08.522
Same shape.

76
00:05:08.522 --> 00:05:14.228
But a Java thread waiting for the lock is blocked, and the same thread may take it twice.

77
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:19.703
So, if only one coroutine runs at a time, how can two lose an addition?

78
00:05:19.703 --> 00:05:23.611
Because one at a time only holds between suspension points.

79
00:05:23.611 --> 00:05:25.797
The pen goes down at every pause.

80
00:05:25.797 --> 00:05:27.784
A Mutex keeps it in your hand.

81
00:05:27.933 --> 00:05:28.735
Quick check.

82
00:05:28.735 --> 00:05:34.478
On a one-thread view, a helper reads the list and writes it, with no pause in between.

83
00:05:34.478 --> 00:05:36.081
Can an addition be lost?

84
00:05:39.133 --> 00:05:39.345
No.

85
00:05:39.345 --> 00:05:43.934
Between suspension points, the view runs one coroutine at a time.

86
00:05:43.934 --> 00:05:46.193
That's why it counted every pea.
